---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const CardAssign = `a ← expression`;
const CardDisplay = `DISPLAY(expression)`;
const CardMath = `a + b    a - b
a * b    a / b
a MOD b`;
const CardRandom = `RANDOM(a, b)`;

const WorkedJs = `let total = 12 + 8;
console.log(total);`;
const WorkedPseudo = `total ← 12 + 8
DISPLAY(total)`;

const One = `let crewCount = 7;
console.log(crewCount);`;

const Two = `let boxes = 47;
let perCrate = 6;
let leftOver = boxes % perCrate;
console.log("Left over:", leftOver);`;

const Three = `let a = 9;
let b = 4;
let c = (a + b) * 2;
console.log("Result:", c);`;

const TraceOne = `x ← 17
y ← 5
DISPLAY(x / y)
DISPLAY(x MOD y)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Pseudocode Challenge: Arithmetic</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Rewrite short JavaScript programs in the notation the AP exam uses.
            No computer needed &mdash; this one works on paper, and doing it on
            paper is closer to the real thing.
          </p>

          <p>
            You will never write this notation in a program. You only need to{" "}
            <i>read</i> it fluently, because that is what the multiple-choice
            section is made of. Translating in this direction is how reading
            gets easy.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Reading and writing assignment as an arrow</li>
            <li>
              Splitting one <b>console.log</b> into separate <b>DISPLAY</b>{" "}
              statements
            </li>
            <li>
              Recognizing <b>MOD</b> as the same operation as <b>%</b>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="reference">
          <h2>What You Are Allowed to Use</h2>

          <p>
            Everything from the exam reference sheet that we have covered so
            far. Nothing else appears in these problems.
          </p>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={CardAssign}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Assignment"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={CardDisplay}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Display"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={CardMath}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Arithmetic"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={CardRandom}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Random"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>Three rules that catch people:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Assignment is <b class="text-sn-theme">←</b>, never{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">=</b>. If you are writing by hand, draw
              the arrow.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">DISPLAY</b> takes one value. Two things
              means two statements.
            </li>
            <li>
              There are <b>no semicolons</b> and no <b>let</b>. A variable comes
              into existence the first time you assign to it.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="worked">
          <h2>Worked Example</h2>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={WorkedJs}
                frame="code"
                lang="javascript"
                title="JavaScript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={WorkedPseudo}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Exam Notation"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two lines in, two lines out. Notice what disappeared:{" "}
            <b>let</b>, the semicolons, and <b>console.log</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Translate These</h2>

          <p>
            Write the exam-notation version of each program. Line for line where
            you can.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>1</h3>

          <Code
            code={One}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Problem 1"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>2</h3>

          <p>
            Careful with the last line. One <b>console.log</b> displaying two
            things does not stay one statement.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Two}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Problem 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>3</h3>

          <p>Keep the parentheses. They mean the same thing in both notations.</p>

          <Code
            code={Three}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Problem 3"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>4 &mdash; The Other Direction</h3>

          <p>
            This one is already in exam notation.{" "}
            <b>Write down exactly what it displays</b>, in order.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={TraceOne}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 4"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Both answers are different numbers, and neither one is a whole-number
            division. Work them out by hand before checking.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Your translations of problems 1, 2, and 3</li>
            <li>The two values displayed by problem 4</li>
            <li>
              One sentence: which part of the translation felt least natural,
              and why?
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Handwritten and photographed is fine, and is the closest thing to
            exam conditions.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
